3.1. Different Group Types in the Community

i. 	Within the Roblox myth community, groups serve as pillars; they share myths, 
gather hunters for events or hunts, and categorize or place myths relative to others. 
Despite this service to the community, there are many negatives to them. A Roblox 
group allows for simple functionality, spread among the ranks in the group. Usually, the 
lowest have no power other than sight and access to rank applications, while the hunter 
route generally grants you access to group walls or shouts, and eventually ranking 
powers. Ranking up as a myth just means that the rankers determine you to fit criteria, 
commonly based on visits or a subjective review. With Roblox groups comes a way to 
advertise as a myth, and a way for people to see where you are relative to others; 
getting higher ranks or getting to be in a shout allows hunters in the group to find you 
and engage in your work. Roblox groups become a liability when it comes to enriching a 
community; there is no conversation between people, even of the same rank, let alone 
communication with higher ranks. Beyond this, relying on shouts and the group wall can 
not uphold a community where there are hundreds of people creating or working on 
myths daily. Roblox groups fail to hold important resources, such as the applications 
needed for ranks, communication for hunters during hunts outside of the game or on the 
group wall, or any large access to what the group is for beyond what is given in the 
about. Roblox groups can’t uphold a community that surrounds hundreds of games.

ii. 	Between Roblox groups and Discord groups associated, they play as radical 
opposites. Roblox groups are lacking in freedom, whereas Discord groups grant too much 
freedom. Myth Discords carry the rest of the Roblox group that Roblox fails to, from 
access to applications, to defining documents, to knowledge of important problematic 
people, providing a place for hunters to collect their findings on the current hunt, and so 
on. Discords allow for great connection between low and high-ranking members, and 
offer a great place for the advertisement of one’s myth. Beyond these surface positives 
lie dark dangers, enough to make lengthy lists of them, the creeps. Recently, as of 
writing this, one of, if not the most important hunters in RMH was revealed to have had 
deplorable relations with a minor as an adult, revealing the greatest disaster of the 
community’s reliance on Discord. It quite simply allows people like this to reach an 
audience that may be susceptible to abuse or manipulation. In this case, he recognized 
little of the deplorable actions shown, then decided to step down, as do many others 
caught, but there is absolutely the possibility to use alternate accounts to continue access 
to the community.

iii. 	The community manages between both Roblox and Discord through friend 
groups, where people gather not for any established group, but for their own shared joy 
of hunting or making myths. Friend groups can do what established groups can do, from 
hunts to documentation to myth interactions, and oftentimes they tend to be skilled; 
friend groups can even be the basis of the origins of established groups. On the myth 
side, myth friend groups gather at social events, share experiences with each other 
relating to their craft or interactions, form new myths or myth groups, and so on. The 
other class of community members away from groups is the outsider, one who is in one 
or two established groups, but tends to spend most of their time in some of the largest 
and most popular myths. Their time in the community is entirely their time in game, 
rather than making friends in the community or finding groups on or off Roblox. This is 
the community in the most innocent case, as there is very little danger to be had when 
away from groups. Groups bring people hungry for power or control; groups bring about 
the exact problem this community needs to deal with, this sharp hierarchy that arises in 
even the smallest groups on or off Roblox.

