3.2. Good and Bad, the Community

i. 	In any community of people, a moral good and bad takes shape with or without 
solid rules set in place. These tend to follow morals from reality, or from other 
communities, but in the myth community specifically, we can glean some unique ideas 
that have formed. One of these is for the interactions from hunters to myths, the 
preservation of the characters, which should entail respect from both sides. The 
interaction should not be interrupted by jokes or one-sided fun or judgment, nor should 
it be engaged with in a way that subverts the intentions or plan of the myth, or of the 
building of the world. Respect comes first, then avoiding breaking immersion, then 
engaging in the story or feeling that is expressed by the myth. For myths to hunters, it 
should be expected that a myth should wish to preserve immersion, which means no 
admin, not straying too far from character, and a myth should not reveal too much, and 
should be well thought out. Aside from in-myth interactions, groups themselves should 
stay professional and open, conversation should be honest and free as well, and there 
should not be too many private conversations that include some potentially dangerous 
power dynamic. The community dies when it starts to connect people who could abuse 
hierarchy or act in very childish ways. Keep in mind that these are virtues; they are 
what increase the quality of the art and the interactions, but are not requirements. 
These are morally good, agreeable to most of the community, so that when disgraced, 
the community’s quality fails, and people lose interest.

ii. 	So what should change? Our morals can be defined, but how have we traced 
them? How should we continue from them? It’s visible that this community has endured 
stagnation for many years, and the creative risks taken have shrunk as well. From the 
perspective of multiple experienced myths,7 the community must not corner itself in 
despair; let people recognize the art of myths and flourish with creativity. Myths want 
the community to be more receptive to different creations and how they vary in concept 
and construction, which means more perspectives can be taken into account. It is also 
imperative that the community recognize the false creativity that is free models, AI, and 
the recycling of ideas from other myths, whether current or past. Artists wish that 
artists portray their truest art, and wish generally for a rise in passion directed to an 
audience. For me, I wish for myths to develop their ideas and messages they wish to push to 
the world much, much more. We can go great distances if we truly dig deep into 
not only real grievances, but larger themes, where perspective matters, where people 
must learn or shift their conceptions.

iii. 	From a hunter’s perspective, I’ve seen the same wishes, along with enhanced 
involvement between hunters and myths. A new idea is that myths could start to include 
hunters in their story as it continues, which has been engaged with before by the Smiles 
family, and aside from that, the general idea for the future of the community is freedom 
of creative choice; a myth should have liberty in the means they choose to present 
themselves to hunters. It must be noted that originality in creation is imperative, and 
that this originality has nothing to do with avoiding the same clichés or types other 
myths have engaged in; not all clown myths have the same idea or story, and the same 
goes for family, trigger, or any other cliché myth. You can find a way to stand out from 
others, even within the same “genre” or classification. On the other hand, some can take 
their wishes or projection to a more pessimistic view; to them, myths are projected to 
stay stagnant in growth. Thousands engage in cheap myth creation or form weak 
characters in the most popular myth games. The future of the community must embrace 
those with more narrow and built ideas, those less popular at this point, but who carry 
strong art. The community then must avoid two simple yet monolithic troubles, 
unoriginality for one, and the result of our anonymity, those perverts and abusers.

