3.3. Downfall, Community-Scaled Betrayal

i. 	We all know the story of the myth community, a golden age followed by a collapse 
led by the corruption of a paragon of the community, and most focus on the latter. To 
me, the several years prior to said downfall must be taken into account, and how the 
community had let such people get into power must be seen. Though rumors and 
legends have been around since the rise of Roblox, the myth community truly began 
around 2010-13, where people began to create accounts specifically for the purpose of 
creating mystery, using their own accounts to form stories and games, and most 
importantly, creating the first groups dedicated to myth hunting. It was a shift from a 
myth-busting concept to a myth-hunting idea, which then culminated in the most 
important myth group within the community, Robloxian Myth Hunters, formed in 2012. 
RMH would build up as a center for myths and hunters alike through the years, 
remaining on Roblox as the community built itself. In the time between the formation of 
the group and the creation of the Discord in late 2016(populated in 2017), another group 
would form to match it from 2014, Roblox’s Myths, which conceptualized their myths 
more as rumors, an SCP-like group. The rise of Discord across the community would 
mark the start of the popularization. From 2017, the community would spread more 
through YouTube, Flamingo would start hunting, and Jokerkid would start pushing the 
community much more. Here, a myth turned from a rarity to a very common thing; 
development groups would form for small myths to build their skills, a concept arose 
that everyone could become a myth, and Discord would be pushed for everybody in the 
community to be connected. Along with the rise of Discord, groups would mold 
themselves to fit popularity and ranking needs, and hierarchy would form where many 
people hold power over many more, mainly having to do with ranking myths or hunters. 
This model of ranking hunters to manage the group would allow many unfit people into 
higher and higher ranks, and maintain power held over others.

ii.	A very common negative for the community, and Roblox itself, is Discord’s 
involvement in every part. Discord pushes people within the community to communicate 
beyond their rank; this is good for group management, but awful for those willing to 
abuse it. A large part of Discord is private messaging, and most, if not all with dark 
motives, will take it there for the freedom it allows power-wise. This is exactly what 
every doc or work notifying of someone’s monstrous actions will publicize: private 
messages that include incriminating or perverse interaction. The result of this freedom 
would form as the community pushed deep into 2020, head held high at the peak of the 
community’s popularity. Kazdam, the leader of the RM group, which had taken the helm 
of the community in the eyes of most, would be found to have been abusing his power 
and reach for the most insidious actions the community had ever seen. At the time, it 
was an unprecedented occurrence; very few members of the community had engaged in 
anything close to such actions, let alone the most powerful man to have ever been in the 
community. Within days, the most populated group within the community would 
collapse, condemned by all high ranks and unrecoverable on all grounds. The downfall 
would follow, as YouTubers fade away, as the most skilled take their capabilities 
elsewhere, as the most powerful bow their heads in disgust, seeking solace elsewhere. 
As the community would continue, held to RMH as a last stand, very few would remain, 
and a new era would rise; one of further degeneracy, despair, and confusion.

iii. 	This collapse had no meager effect; nearly every part of the community outside of 
Roblox or Discord would fall away, groups were left abandoned, and life in the 
community would falter. Now, the myth community has reached a stage where old myth 
groups are no longer trying to salvage what they had, no myths are seriously returning, 
at least at any relevant rate. In the years following, many may return from retirement 
to reform, maybe just to keep their games up to date, maybe to try and continue, but 
none would have any notable impact, nor would they stay for a considerable amount of 
time. Visible shrinkage of rank requirements would take place, and along with this 
desperate clinging to what had been, a new rise in corruption would sweep across the 
community; tens more were found to be perpetrating the same acts which had led this 
community to this dark place. What would end up taking place would be an emigration of 
the younger, the community aged up as those less connected community members fell 
away. The community is stagnant now, grasping at what it has left, though it remains 
with very, very few people. The largest events may pass 100 players, Discords remain 
relatively silent, and from experience, there is an extreme lack of myths; the largest lists 
of active myths rarely breach 3 digits, and even less if you factor out older myths. Even 
with what happened, it is difficult to explain such a large decline, from hundreds of 
thousands to potential bare thousands.

