3.4. Thoughts on a Future

i. 	This is a tough place to be in; nobody is motivated, and those who are end up 
putting it into art rather than any movement for change. In the most recent summer of 
2025, a surge in community members was notable in RMH, perhaps associated with the 
rise of a popular game, Forsaken, as it carried characters from the myth community. It’s 
hard to say whether this truly pushed people into the community; Forsaken remains as 
popular as ever, while the community has returned to a stagnant, constant small inflow 
of people. Even with this rise in membership, however, very little has changed; perhaps 
the market of visits has expanded. The community’s best bet for popularity is to get any 
somewhat popular YouTuber into the community for at least some time, maybe connect 
to other Roblox games or art online, but what we can be sure of is that the community 
will live on, whether hundreds or hundreds of thousands, and I’ll be glad to be part of it 
in any case. I believe the community will stay stagnant, and though the world seems to 
work in cycles, I do remain steadfast with my idea that the myth community has broken 
the cycle; even summers don’t usually bring in a lot more players, no matter how far the 
community is, there is always someone willing to abuse power within it, no matter how 
old the community ages up to. With a lack of new players, the overall age of the 
community will rise, along with skill and potential outreach. One can only hope.

ii. 	An idea for the community is to push beyond the boundaries we lie within; what 
can we do when away from Roblox or Discord? On TikTok, there is a small part of the 
community, garnering at some points tens of thousands of views. Though it has a 
presence, it generally entails nostalgia or the most popular myths, perhaps less so 
associated with the community itself. The myths presented end up on the lower end, 
quality-wise and character-wise, matching the generally young audience. Aside from 
TikTok, Reddit appears as a tiny part of the community, confused and rare, speaking 
into another void. YouTube, a lasting site connected to the community, remains for the 
rest of the creators, the largest and closest to the biggest groups and myths. At the 
community’s peak, we had focused on one specific creator, whereas now our attention is 
put on the rest. The community needs to expand, on Roblox it remains either stagnant 
or in decay, and garnering attention from other sites or communities on the internet can 
be a ticket to popularization. At the moment, we seem to focus on YouTube, yet there is 
great potential in TikTok. We must push outside of Roblox for the sake of our future.

