3.5. Relation to Other Art Communities

i. 	In seeing this rise and sharp collapse, the potential for the community is still 
great. Seeking communities where popularity fluctuated similarly to ours was quite the 
challenge, Roblox myths truly are like no other art form. In my research, I saw comic 
book art, tattooing, and street art, as well as avant-garde art within art forms. What was 
common for most was a collapse from some scandal, some assumption from the general 
public that the art was a danger to children or the mind itself, or to war efforts, and yet, 
all had prevailed past their stages of death. What commonly occurred with this sudden 
prevailing was the public’s association with the art as nostalgic, something which has 
already begun slowly for us, along with a connection with some rebranding to avoid the 
assumptions of most. These communities found their positives and unique ways, and 
portrayed them with rising publicity, street art turned from vandalism to Banksy, comic 
books turned from a threat of child delinquency to the opposite with films adapting 
them, and painters with an anti-war stance before the Second World War had ended up 
watching society match their ideals over time. I believe our future rests still in our 
hands; we must shift the community in some direction if we wish to return to the heights 
once so common. This route of redemption could mean a redefinition of the community 
and the art within.

ii. 	The obvious connection between these art forms and ours is a loss of merit among 
outsiders; I've seen many who are reminded of the community wish to return, only to be 
stopped immediately by the thought of what took the community down. They’re 
reminded of the downfall, and are of the belief that the community is fully dead, without 
any players. Whether or not you believe that this modern, smaller community is better 
for the quality of myths, our unpopularity will be grating for the rest of our time at this 
scale. Those art forms that achieved redemption had retained their culture and norms; 
there is no need to shift the community itself, just the perception of the community. 
Though we can not relate to any legal crackdown on the spread of our art, we have had a 
similar fallback to the underground levels of Roblox. To elaborate on what should be 
done, most of those communities had reframed themselves through renaming; graffiti 
turned into street art, comics turned into graphic novels. For myths, there have already 
been attempts to rename them, but it is clear that this is not an option due to the 
immovability of the community in its fractured state. To go with this, perhaps we should 
wait for the rest of Roblox to “catch up” with our myth culture; surely they will 
eventually wish for something myth-like once more. What stands out to me, though, is 
the unique potential for myths to make their way into other media; our options being 
ARGs and rumors for the sake of the community. We can make our way into these other, 
more popular communities to build our notoriety, and then work towards greater 
connection with those communities to latch on to their popularity, bringing many more 
to this community.
